# TEXTURE-3-R5 §F — CHECKSUM-PINNED BUILD INPUTS MUST NOT BE REWRITTEN ON CHECKOUT.
#
# The repository is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true and carried no .gitattributes, so Git
# translated LF to CRLF for every file it guessed was text. For ordinary source that is invisible. For a
# VENDORED INPUT PINNED BY SHA-256 it is not: the checked-out bytes stop being the bytes that were pinned,
# and the guard that exists to detect a substituted upstream file fails on a file nobody touched.
#
# Measured before this line was written, on a clean checkout of 5fc0249:
#
#   tools/geo/data/cldr-46-subdivisions-zh_Hant.xml   committed 458 B      checked out 472 B      (+14 CR)
#   tools/geo/data/wikidata-admin1-zh.json            committed 360,992 B  checked out 364,668 B  (+3,676 CR)
#
# Exactly one byte per line, and the committed blob's digest e8685bde… is the digest the asset pins. So the
# vendored data was never wrong and the pins were never wrong — the checkout was. That is worth fixing at the
# cause rather than by relaxing the assertion, because relaxing it would delete the only check that would
# notice a genuinely substituted source.
#
# Scoped deliberately to the vendored inputs. A repository-wide `* -text` would restate the line endings of
# every file in the tree, which is a much larger change than the defect justifies.
tools/geo/data/** -text

# TEXTURE-3-R8 §A — A CONTENT-DERIVED CACHE TOKEN CANNOT SURVIVE A CHECKOUT THAT REWRITES THE CONTENT.
#
# R8 pins the runtime URL of the lazily-injected ADM1 asset to its own SHA-256, so that a changed asset is a
# changed URL and no browser can serve stale division boundaries. That only works if "the bytes" is one
# answer. Measured on this checkout, it was two:
#
#   assets/js/data/world-admin1-10m.js   committed 594,783 B   checked out 594,791 B   (+8 CR, one per line)
#
#   committed / served by GitHub Pages   sha256 4d61535a9116c49939db112ed0ce9c8820fd22f898f1e430af60748bbca93bab
#   checked out on this Windows clone    sha256 bd17f93866e057804a385d9c608bae85ed9ee37665848d21dc8088704739464f
#
# The token must name what the BROWSER fetches, which is the committed blob. Deriving it from the working copy
# instead would make the identity a property of the developer's core.autocrlf setting rather than of the asset —
# the same token would be "wrong" on Windows and "right" in CI, and whoever noticed would correct the token to
# match their own disk and silently break the deployed URL.
#
# So the file is excluded from translation and its working copy restored to the committed bytes. Disk, blob and
# deployed asset are now one digest, and `sha256sum assets/js/data/world-admin1-10m.js` reproduces the token on
# any platform. The R8 suite additionally asserts the file carries NO CR byte, so a clone that loses this rule
# fails loudly instead of minting a token for bytes nobody serves.
#
# Scoped to this one asset, for the reason the rule above is scoped: four other files in assets/js/data share the
# same latent divergence, but none of them is pinned by content, so renormalizing them is a larger change than
# R8's defect justifies. Whichever one is pinned next should be added to this line, not discovered again.
assets/js/data/world-admin1-10m.js -text
